<h2>The litigation so far</h2>

<p>The wife's application under Section 12 of the DV Act (Ct. Cases 2271/2020) yielded an interim maintenance order from the Trial Court on 28 February 2023. Both sides took the dispute to the appellate court under Section 29, which partially modified the order on 1 April 2024 in Criminal Appeal No. 45/2023. The husband then invoked the Delhi High Court's jurisdiction in CRL.M.C. 5097/2024, arguing that the very foundation — domestic violence — was absent, and that the allegations against him and his family were false. Dr. Justice Swarana Kanta Sharma reserved judgment on 3 February 2026 and pronounced it on 4 April 2026.</p>

<h2>The prima facie gate</h2>

<p>The Court's approach separates two questions that respondents routinely conflate. Whether the allegations are true is the trial's business. Whether the allegations, assuming them for the moment, amount in law to domestic violence is the interim court's business — and it is a low, definitional gate, not a mini-trial. The wife's allegations included physical assault, verbal abuse and humiliation within the household, and inappropriate and obscene conduct by the husband's brother towards her. Taken at face value, the Court held, such allegations fall within the broad contours of domestic violence as defined under the PWDV Act, and the wife therefore prima facie satisfied the requirements of an aggrieved person under Section 2(a). Whether they are ultimately established can be finally decided only during trial.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Section 3 is wide</h3><p>Physical, verbal, emotional and economic abuse all qualify; conduct of the respondent's relatives within the shared household can form part of the pattern.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Face-value review</h3><p>At the interim stage the court assumes the allegations and tests them against the definition — it does not weigh rival affidavits for truth.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Counter-allegations</h3><p>Bald and unsubstantiated allegations against the aggrieved person do not defeat interim maintenance; disputed documents await evidence.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Trial decides</h3><p>Everything held prima facie remains open; the final adjudication under Section 12 is untouched by the interim findings.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Money: the Rajnesh v. Neha discipline</h2>

<p>On quantum, the judgment is a careful application of Rajnesh v. Neha, (2021) 2 SCC 324. Interim maintenance is ordinarily payable from the date of filing of the application — here, 20 October 2020 — rather than from the date of separation. And receipts are not ignored: the wife had received Rs. 20,00,000 from the sale of a property, and the Court directed that this sum be adjusted against the monthly interim maintenance of Rs. 25,000 computed from the application date, with the adjustment running until 20 June 2027, whereafter the husband pays consolidated maintenance of Rs. 25,000 per month. The structure respects both sides of the ledger: the wife's entitlement accrues from the date she sought relief, and the husband's liability is credited with what she has already received from matrimonial assets.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Application filed</h3><p>Section 12 DV application with interim prayer under Section 23 (20.10.2020).</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Interim order</h3><p>Magistrate fixes monthly interim maintenance (28.02.2023); appeal partly modifies it (01.04.2024).</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>High Court review</h3><p>Prima facie domestic violence affirmed; lump-sum receipt of Rs. 20 lakh adjusted against arrears.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Trial continues</h3><p>Truth of the allegations, and final relief under Sections 12, 18 to 22, remain for evidence.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Reading the decision from both chairs</h2>

<p>For aggrieved persons, the judgment confirms that the DV Act's interim machinery does not stall merely because every allegation is denied or countered: a coherent set of allegations within Section 3 unlocks interim protection, and attacks on her character or conduct must await proof. For respondents, the useful lessons are financial rather than definitional. The decision shows that substantial sums already received by the aggrieved person from family assets are legitimately brought into account, and that the date-of-application rule cuts both ways — it curbs claims reaching back to separation, while making delay in deciding the application expensive. Both sides, finally, should note what the High Court refused to do: convert a Section 29 appeal and a petition against interim orders into a premature trial of the marriage.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Interim orders under the DV Act are temporary arrangements, revisable under Section 25 on change of circumstances and subsumed in the final order under Section 12. Nothing in an interim finding — for either side — predetermines the outcome of the trial, and courts repeatedly caution against treating the interim stage as the main battle.</p></div>
